  {
    "id": "2109986538415169562",
    "text": "OPEC has cut its 2026 oil demand growth forecast to just 180,000 barrels per day (kb/d), while raising its 2027 projection to 2.55 million barrels per day (mb/d), pointing to a sharp recovery in consumption next year. The organisation also reported a 920 kb/d increase in September OPEC production to 25.1 mb/d, approaching pre-war levels, as higher output from Saudi Arabia and Iraq more than offset declines elsewhere.\n\nHowever, a substantial gap remains between OPEC's relatively optimistic outlook and the considerably more bearish assessments from the EIA and IEA. In its latest Short-Term Energy Outlook, the EIA projected a 2 mb/d contraction in demand this year, compared with the 2.5 mb/d decline forecast by the IEA in September.\n\nThe divergence highlights the exceptional uncertainty surrounding the demand impact of the Middle East disruption, particularly the extent to which elevated energy prices, supply shortages and weaker economic activity are destroying consumption. Attention now turns to the IEA's October Oil Market Report, due tomorrow, for an updated assessment of both demand destruction and the recovery in Middle East supplies.",
    "createdAt": "2026-10-13T12:36:07+00:00",
    "tweetTime": 1791894967000,
    "username": "Ole_S_Hansen",
    "displayName": "Ole S Hansen",
